Title: Load a Widevine CDM from --widevine-cdm-path
Origin: electron-legacy (this repo)

Lets Electron load the Widevine CDM, so encrypted media (Netflix, Hulu, Spotify)
reaches the decryptor instead of failing at
navigator.requestMediaKeySystemAccess(). Needs `enable_widevine = true` in the
GN args, which assets/args/*.gn sets.

No CDM is bundled and none is downloaded: there is no component updater in
Electron (BrowserProcessImpl::component_updater() returns nullptr), so the CDM
has to be supplied with --widevine-cdm-path. Nothing is registered without it,
which keeps a build that was never pointed at a CDM behaving exactly as before.

Two directory layouts are accepted:

  - A Chromium component directory: manifest.json at the top level, library
    under _platform_specific/win_x64/ (or win_x86/). The manifest is
    authoritative, so codecs, encryption schemes and the version all come from
    the CDM itself, and --widevine-cdm-version is not needed. This is the
    layout Chrome's own component updater leaves behind, so the CDM can be
    copied straight out of a Chrome install:
      .../Google/Chrome/Application/<ver>/WidevineCdm
  - A flat directory holding just widevinecdm.dll, which is what Electron's
    --widevine-cdm-path has always meant. There is no manifest, so the
    capability is stated in code and --widevine-cdm-version must give the
    CDM's version. That stated list includes HEVC, which Chromium only offers
    where a platform decoder backs it; this fork software-decodes it, so
    encrypted HEVC is worth advertising even on a machine with no HEVC support
    of its own.

Because enable_widevine_cdm_host_verification requires is_chrome_branded, these
builds do no VMP (Verified Media Path) signing, and without a VMP signature a
CDM is only trusted at Widevine security level 3. Sites that insist on L1 are
expected to refuse to play; this is for testing, and for sites that accept L3.
castlabs' fork (https://github.com/castlabs/electron-releases) is the option for
anyone who needs real VMP signing.

Most of what this touches is not new code but dead code. Electron's Widevine
support has always been behind BUILDFLAG(ENABLE_WIDEVINE), which is false
unless is_chrome_branded, so none of it has been compiled in years and all of
it had rotted past the point of building:

  - media::EncryptionMode became media::EncryptionScheme
  - media::VideoCodec::kCodecVP8 and friends lost their kCodec prefix
  - media::CdmProxy, and so CdmProxy::Protocol, were deleted outright
  - content::CdmCapability moved to media::CdmCapability, and its video codec
    list became a std::map<VideoCodec, VideoCodecInfo> rather than a vector
  - content::CdmInfo's constructor is now
    (key_system, Robustness, capability, supports_sub_key_systems, name,
    CdmType, path), where it used to take a GUID and a file system id
  - kWidevineCdmGuid and kWidevineCdmFileSystemId no longer exist; a single
    media::CdmType kWidevineCdmType replaced both
  - widevine_cdm_common.h, which declares every one of those constants, was
    never included in the first place

Registration is therefore rewritten against the current APIs, following
CreateCdmInfoFromWidevineDirectory() in //chrome/common/media/cdm_registration.cc
so the component path behaves the way Chrome's does. The old code also only
assigned *cdm_path on the first call while caching its "found" answer in a
static, so a second call reported an available CDM at an empty path; reading the
path out of the cache instead fixes that.

The renderer half was never wired up at all, which is the part that would still
have failed even after the browser side compiled. renderer_client_base.cc
included chrome/renderer/media/chrome_key_systems.h under ENABLE_WIDEVINE but
never called GetChromeKeySystems(), and RendererClientBase did not override
ContentRendererClient::GetSupportedKeySystems(), so Blink was never told the
registered CDM existed. GetChromeKeySystems() is a four-line wrapper around
cdm::GetSupportedKeySystemsUpdates() whose only addition is passing
can_persist_data=false for incognito profiles -- and only persistent-license
support reads that, which this CDM does not advertise -- so Electron calls
//components/cdm/renderer directly and takes no dependency on chrome/renderer.

//components/cdm/common and //components/cdm/renderer both restrict visibility
to the embedders Chromium ships, so //electron/* is added to those two lists.
electron/chromium_src already DEPed //components/cdm/renderer under
enable_widevine, so that dep could never have resolved before this.

The same stale block in electron/chromium_src/BUILD.gn is removed. It compiled
four chrome/renderer/media sources, two of which -- chrome_key_systems_provider
.cc and .h -- no longer exist: the ChromeKeySystemsProvider class they held was
replaced by the KeySystemSupportRegistration mechanism this patch uses, so with
enable_widevine on, gn produced a target for a file that is not there:

  ninja: error: '../../chrome/renderer/media/chrome_key_systems_provider.cc',
  needed by 'obj/electron/chromium_src/chrome/chrome_key_systems_provider.obj',
  missing and no known rule to make it

Deleting just those two lines would swap that for a link error. The surviving
chrome_key_systems.cc includes chrome/renderer/process_state.h for its one
incognito check, and process_state is not in chromium_src, so
process_state::IsIncognitoProcess() would go undefined. Nothing in Electron
calls GetChromeKeySystems() either -- its only caller is
chrome_content_renderer_client.cc, which Electron does not build -- so the
whole block goes, and the dep moves to electron_lib where the call actually is.

